//BUG="float literals reach a builtin as C integer constants, so 1.0 / 2.0 is 0"
//OUT="0.500000000"

// The defect of 52 at ordinary magnitudes. Codegen prints a float constant
// with `format!("{x}")` (src/compiler/codegen/mod.rs), which drops the
// fractional part of a whole number: 1.0 is emitted as `1` and 2.0 as `2`.
// The builtin is emitted inline as a C operator, so the generated expression
// is
//
//     (1 / 2)
//
// — integer division. This prints 0.000000000.
//
// `1.0 / 2.0` written with the operator is unaffected, but only by accident:
// `operator/` is a function, its parameters are declared `double`, and
// passing the integer constants converts them before the division happens.
// The builtins are the one place a literal meets a C operator directly,
// which is where 11_builtins/17 already uses float literals for comparisons
// (where the missing `.0` does not change the answer).
//
// 55 is the same loss of type for i64 literals.

fn main
    out "{_builtin_div[f64](1.0 2.0)}"
